From dd3dcfe081b93439f2c11be06dfad38887f230bc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 14:34:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] fix(update): settle the restart obligation for an
 unmapped stop on a live home and for external-checkout runtimes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Problem: since 2026-11-03 13:00 UTC every `hermes` CLI call on hermes-hub printed
"A previous `hermes update` pulled new code but did not restart running gateways", although the
only checkout-backed gateway (default, pid 2542475) was current on ceff8ab7358f. The host
obligation (gateway-locks/host-update-restart-*.json, expected_sha ceff8ab7358f) owed:
default, askeve-builder, askeve-reviewer (both code 14a1244773e6, askeve-runtime's own pinned
core) and an unmapped row {profile: None, pid: 2549548, stopped_unmapped: true}.

Why 1: `_marker_only_restart_obsolete()` returned False, so `_update_owes_fleet_restart()` kept
  warning. Replaying that inventory against the 13:00 fleet (default current, both askeve rows
  `external`): the askeve rows ARE covered (`_fleet_covered_gateways` counts external rows and the
  current-check skips them); the unmapped row is what fails: `("gateway","")` needs a live local
  gateway that no owed profile explains, and default is explained by its own row.
Why 2: pid 2549548 was recorded as unmapped because `_restart_manual_gateways` SIGTERMed a
  `gateway run` process on a home in this update's scope that held no profile's gateway.pid and
  was no unit MainPID ("Stopped 1 manual gateway process(es)", update.log; receipt killed_pids
  [2549548]). It started after 12:58:43 UTC and never logged `gateway.start` in any home's
  gateway-exit-diag.log, so it was a pre-lock / lock-losing `gateway run`, not a serving gateway.
Why 3: the row carries no identity: the stop recorded only the pid, not the home it ran on, so no
  successor can ever be matched to it; on a host whose every home already has its one
  lock-holding gateway (gateway.lock is per home) the debt is undischargeable.
Why 4 (askeve rows): `_record_owed_gateway_inventory` records every planned gateway, including
  profiles whose runtime serves ANOTHER checkout; they settle only while their external row is
  live, so an Ask Eve gateway restarting or down for its own reasons holds this checkout's
  warning hostage.
Root (happened): the owed inventory records identities it can never prove (an unmapped stop with
  no home) and debts this checkout cannot pay (external-checkout runtimes).
Root (not caught): the unmapped-row tests (test_update_fleet_check_fail_closed.py) only model a
  stop whose successor is a NEW profile gateway; none models the common case of a stray
  `gateway run` on a home whose gateway is already current, and no test put an external-checkout
  runtime in the owed inventory.
Recurrence: first time for this chain; related unmapped-debt rules came from codemap §6 V7/V12.

Fix:
- the restart sweep and the stale-survivor sweep read each unmapped gateway's home BEFORE the
  signal (`gateway_pid_home`: live environ, else the spawn ledger) into
  `_GatewayRestartOutcome.stopped_unmapped_homes`; the owed row keeps it as `home`;
- `_marker_owed_gateways` owes such a row to the profile whose home it ran on, so that home's
  live current gateway settles it; an unreadable home keeps the old fail-closed rule;
- `_record_owed_gateway_inventory` no longer records profiles the post-restart fleet snapshot
  shows `external`.
A stale or down checkout-backed gateway, and an unmapped stop on a home with no live gateway,
still owe the restart (control cases in the new test file, green before and after).

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_update_obligation_row_classes.py — 4 cases RED on main
(external row recorded/undischargeable, unmapped-on-live-home undischargeable, both sweeps lose
the home), 3 controls green on both.
